	('c6779a68-6c28-405f-9df7-cd4db19dbdb0', '57be074b-d837-43b9-a5a1-6f3959cdea53', NULL, 'Based on your conversation with Mike, so excited that you''re working with voice agents.', 'idea', '2025-03-21 20:47:39.872185+00', '2025-03-21 20:47:39.872185+00', NULL, NULL),
	('9518dc9a-dac3-47bc-a0f2-528f754d4f17', '713b8455-cc3b-4ab1-bcfb-255f93bf56b0', NULL, '📘 Artifact from Jeff & Jeff’s Upduo Session
Conversation Date: March 13, 2025
 Participants: Jeff Ream & Jeff Crawford
 Session Topic: Innovation in AI – Recent Updates
 Length: 29 minutes, 52 seconds
 Captured by Insight Fly 2025

🧭 Narrative Reflection: Hot Lava & Human Prompts
In a session filled with warmth, wit, and genuine curiosity, Jeff Ream and Jeff Crawford explored the intersection of AI and education—not through technical jargon, but through human experience and imaginative problem-solving.
Ream, a school counselor, imagined his AI superpower as being a “super prompter”—someone who instinctively knows what to ask to get the best outcome. Crawford immediately recognized this as more than an aspiration—it was a reflection of Ream’s years of lived expertise in student-centered dialogue. That moment revealed something deeper: the art of prompting isn’t new—it’s just shifting tools.
Crawford countered with his own vivid metaphor: “hot lava”—AI used to melt away systemic barriers in education. It was a call for justice-oriented tech use, one that champions equitable access for students and liberates teacher creativity.
Together, they reflected on tools like Lovable, Claude, and Perplexity, not as gimmicks, but as extensions of their values. Ream shared efforts to recreate a beloved time-tracking tool using Lovable, bridging past practices with emerging platforms. Crawford emphasized purposeful learning over tool overload, recommending no more than five trusted tools, used deeply.
The two also wrestled honestly with limitations—especially around student data privacy. Ream named the “kneecapping” effect of not being able to run robust data analyses through open AI models. Crawford echoed the need to navigate this space responsibly and strategically.
In the end, they arrived at something powerful: that professional learning doesn’t need to be static, cookie-cutter, or behind the curve. With intention, co-thinking, and the right tools, it can be dynamic, relevant, and responsive to the pace of change—just like this very conversation.

🗣️ Notable Quotes (Verbatim)
“Maybe... to be a super prompter.”
 — Jeff Ream, envisioning his AI superpower and revealing his deep curiosity for crafting the right questions
“You''re probably a super prompter—like epic proportions superpower—when it comes to knowing the right questions to ask today with kids.”
 — Jeff Crawford, affirming the human skill behind Ream’s tech curiosity
“I want hot lava powers with AI to help break through all that stuff.”
 — Jeff Crawford, reframing AI as a force for dismantling systemic barriers
“It’s hard to keep up… Even just ChatGPT—3 different models have come out… so much has developed, even in the last six months.”
 — Jeff Ream, voicing the challenge of rapid tech evolution in education
“What’s possible now is that we can keep growing and doing professional learning in a different way... not stagnant, but dynamic.”
 — Jeff Crawford, envisioning a responsive, human-centered model for professional learning
“I learned that I''m going to play with Perplexity—and think about how I can use it for my work and personal life.”
 — Jeff Ream, marking a clear next step in his learning journey

💭 Self-Reflection Prompts
🔷 Jeff Ream (Counselor | Tinkerer | Prompter)
How does your counseling expertise shape the way you prompt and engage with AI?


What tool or insight from this session do you want to prototype next—and why?


How can you ethically explore data analysis with anonymized patterns or synthetic student profiles?


🔷 Jeff Crawford (Facilitator | Systems Thinker | Connector)
What’s one educational barrier you’re ready to “melt” with hot lava energy—and who needs to be at that table with you?


In what ways are you already modeling dynamic, human-centered PD? What’s next?


What’s one “I want” or “I need” you’re hearing from educators that could shape the Sideby platform?



🛠️ Challenge Invitations (Optional Next Steps)
🧪 Challenge 1: Co-Build a Human-First Tool
Use Lovable (or another platform) to co-design a prototype that reflects student/family planning needs without relying on sensitive data. Test how your human-centered prompting can guide thoughtful design.
→ Anchor Question: What might a tool look like that’s both intuitive and respectful of data boundaries?
🔥 Challenge 2: Hot Lava in Action
Pick one system-level barrier (tech equity, PD structure, etc.) and use a trusted AI tool to:
Research innovative approaches,


Generate strategies,


Create a visual or prompt-based prototype. Bring your findings to your next Upduo or team meeting.


→ Anchor Question: What might change if we stop working around the barriers—and start melting them?

Thank you, Jeff & Jeff, for modeling what it means to learn in dialogue, with curiosity and care.
 You’re not just keeping up—you’re shaping what’s next.', 'idea', '2025-03-28 17:54:07.880936+00', '2025-03-28 17:54:07.880936+00', NULL, NULL),
	('5e0dd81c-40f1-4ef7-8259-d1fa98cccc57', '482f5330-4f63-4232-ab68-d138579256ab', NULL, 'From your reflection transcript: 

"Of Guts, Growth, and Generations: Mikey’s Reflection on AI in Education"
In this solo reflection, Mikey Mendelson invites us into a vulnerable and thoughtful exploration of the tensions educators feel around the rising presence of AI in learning spaces. With characteristic candor and metaphor-rich storytelling, Mikey names both the real and misplaced fears he sees bubbling up in the profession—and begins to sketch out a more hopeful path forward.

At the heart of Mikey’s thinking is a deeply human concern: what do we lose when we let tools think for us? Drawing on the analogy of self-driving cars, he poses a powerful question about skill erosion: if learners never have to "drive" in adverse conditions—whether that means writing under pressure, grappling with a blank page, or solving complex problems unaided—what capacities might remain undeveloped?

But he doesn’t stop at fear. In fact, Mikey gently repositions fear not as a stopping point, but as an invitation to investigate. Is this fear of loss justified? Or are we, perhaps, overlooking the ways in which humanity has always adapted, invented, and grown precisely by offloading tasks to tools?

His historical reference to the Donner Party—harsh survival then vs. smooth travel now—serves as more than a grim anecdote. It''s a mirror for how technological evolution has continually reshaped what we consider necessary knowledge, and how, in doing so, it often expands access, safety, and possibility.

The conversation deepens as Mikey moves from fear to bias—a central concern in AI systems. With clarity and care, he distinguishes between individual prejudice and systemic bias. Rather than shying away from AI’s limitations, he sees them as teachable moments: opportunities to illuminate structural injustices and help learners engage critically with the systems shaping their world.

In Mikey’s words, “Acknowledge it. Teach it. Trust.” These three imperatives capture his stance: a commitment to transparency, education, and intergenerational faith. He imagines an educational future where confronting bias is not a one-time corrective, but a cultural muscle—something students inherit, exercise, and improve upon over time.

Underneath his analysis is a quiet but powerful hope: that the work of teaching isn''t to reject new tools out of fear, but to wield them wisely, helping learners see both their potential and their pitfalls.

Mikey’s reflection surfaces an emerging throughline: not “AI or no AI,” but “better with than without”—provided we stay courageous, curious, and committed to justice.', 'idea', '2025-03-29 00:48:18.801852+00', '2025-03-29 00:48:18.801852+00', NULL, NULL),
	('10c5bad8-44ed-4c29-892d-4513e276cdd8', '369c68b2-4de2-4ba9-884c-a26d3e5a426d', NULL, 'From your reflection transcript:
Narrative Insight: Dale Berry''s Learning Journey
In this brief but heartfelt introduction, Dale Berry offers a vivid snapshot of a professional life anchored in purpose, passion, and deep commitment to growth. As a special education teacher in Madison, Wisconsin, Dale’s voice carries the quiet pride of someone who has not only served in schools but helped build one from the ground up. His story begins with a founding role at an EL Education school in Nevada City, California, where he shaped an Adventure Field Work program that continues to thrive—a testament to the enduring power of vision-driven teaching.

Now rooted in Madison, Dale names joy and support as core to his teaching practice. His tone is reflective and steady, revealing a practitioner who is both grounded and evolving. His entry into this professional community is framed by curiosity and humility: “I’m here to learn more… and to share some of the things that I’ve learned.” This balance between offering and receiving speaks volumes about his sense of reciprocal learning.

Dale positions himself as an “adult learner,” someone who embraces daily growth not out of obligation, but out of love—for his students, for his craft, and for what education can become. His self-awareness is striking: he names empowerment through learning as a source of happiness, and links that joy directly to how he shows up for students. “When I’m learning… I feel like I’m at my happiest,” he says, underscoring the human heartbeat at the center of his work.

Emerging Themes & Highlights
Identity as a Learner: Dale models a growth mindset, emphasizing ongoing learning as a joyful and necessary part of his role.

Legacy and Pride: His foundational work in EL Education speaks to vision, innovation, and lasting impact.

Purpose-Driven Practice: Supporting students who need extra help is not just a job—it’s clearly a calling.

Learning as Empowerment: He connects adult learning to emotional well-being and student success, echoing a belief that professional growth fuels deeper connection in the classroom.', 'idea', '2025-03-29 00:49:31.388377+00', '2025-03-29 00:49:31.388377+00', NULL, NULL),
	('6b62d526-20c4-4850-b45f-5ce954470eda', '369c68b2-4de2-4ba9-884c-a26d3e5a426d', NULL, 'From your chat with Jeff:

"It Still Needs Your Amazing Brain" — A Conversation on AI, Differentiation, and Learner Agency
In their peer session, educators Jeff Crawford and Dale Berry explored the growing influence of AI tools on teaching and learning—not with fear or blind enthusiasm, but with thoughtful curiosity, creativity, and a deep sense of purpose. What emerged from their exchange was not just a technical discussion about tools, but a meaningful inquiry into identity, agency, and the evolving role of both educators and students in the AI era.

From the outset, both educators grounded their conversation in imaginative visions of the future—citing Dune and The Matrix—as metaphors for disruption, scarcity, and the power of mind-machine interaction. That imaginative thread ran through the entire dialogue as they unpacked how AI is not replacing them, but rather amplifying their ability to differentiate, personalize, and remain present with students.

“I’m able to differentiate it in such a fast and accurate way... that then allows my students to have different entry points into that new learning.” – Dale Berry

Dale described how building custom GPTs has radically transformed his workflow—streamlining compliance documentation, scaffolding differentiated tasks in real time, and creating more space for connection. He emphasized not just the tools, but the training—carefully curating data sources (e.g., Wisconsin standards, EL Education principles), shaping tone, and teaching the AI to reflect values of accessibility and student-centeredness. In this way, Dale''s work echoes the deeper stance of Universal Design for Learning (UDL): leveraging flexible, responsive supports that evolve with student needs.

Jeff brought in a humanizing counterbalance, reflecting on how AI is not inherently transformative—it becomes powerful in the hands of learners who are empowered to direct it:

“How do I use these tools… to help me do what I want to do to reach my bigger project?” – Jeff Crawford

He painted a vivid picture of learners using AI not passively, but as designers—building assistants to support authentic passions, like engineering skateboard ramps. This reflects a shift from passive recipients of instruction to active project-based learners, aligning with deeper learning practices and the EL Core Practices of student ownership and real-world application.

Both educators returned often to mindset—the often invisible threshold between resistance and possibility. They noted that while students may adapt quickly to AI tools, many adults are slowed by fear, uncertainty, or legacy models of compliance-driven teaching. But they refused a deficit framing, instead offering encouragement and clarity:

“It still needs your amazing brains to ask it the right questions... It is a tool, like a tool in your tool belt.” – Dale Berry

“We are built to connect with other humans… I know how to support productive struggle, to ask purposeful questions, to facilitate meaningful discourse.” – Jeff Crawford

In these reflections, we see a deliberate honoring of educators'' humanity. AI, they argue, cannot replicate the emotional nuance, contextual insight, or moral presence of a teacher who knows their students deeply. The role of the educator expands—not shrinks—in an AI-supported world, becoming more about designing environments, modeling inquiry, and walking alongside learners.

They closed their session by naming what they learned from each other—Dale appreciating Jeff’s story of a student who used AI as a self-directed music tutor, and Jeff valuing Dale’s reminder to iterate GPTs with feedback, treating them not as fixed tools but evolving partners in learning.

Themes That Emerged:
AI as Differentiation Partner: Enables rapid, nuanced access points for students with varying needs.

Ownership and Agency: Both for educators (customizing GPTs) and students (designing AI tools for real-world tasks).

Mindset Shifts: Especially for adults, reframing AI from threat to creative partner.

Human Connection: Teaching as fundamentally relational—supporting struggle, asking questions, building trust.

Iterative Design: AI tools are most effective when continuously refined through educator feedback and alignment with core values.

Memorable Quotes:
“It still needs your amazing brains.” – Dale Berry
“We see so much more data than an AI tool sees.” – Jeff Crawford
“That mindset… of what it can do and what it can help me achieve—I think that’s a big shift for adults.” – Dale Berry', 'idea', '2025-03-29 00:51:46.743759+00', '2025-03-29 00:51:46.743759+00', NULL, NULL),
	('b8f3a702-8011-4f95-a490-950e0aa66269', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, 'Test idea posted', 'idea', '2025-03-24 19:59:20.228105+00', '2025-03-25 14:34:58.976295+00', 1, 3),
	('ea9c7b5c-d925-4398-9bb7-ef242ab44e07', 'ea155ac4-eb3a-4e24-a4be-84f0143f4348', NULL, 'Educator Reflection: Mike Mendelson
Date: March 21, 2025
 Reflection Length: 54 seconds
 Core Themes: Joy in learning, teacher identity, school culture, community connection

🎙️ Mike’s Moment
"Oh, school was awesome today. There was a rally, and I was in the dance for Dancing Queen with all of my fellow teachers. Then we played basketball. And man, I was feeling like—people who think school is not something they should send their kids to… that really surprises me."
In under a minute, Mike paints a vivid picture of a school day that pulses with energy, connection, and joy. What might seem like a lighthearted moment on the surface actually reveals something much deeper: a vision of school as a place where people show up with care, where fun and belonging matter, and where educators are active members of a joyful community.

🔍 What This Tells Us About Mike’s Teaching and Beliefs
🌱 He sees school as a place worth showing up for.
Mike isn’t just describing events—he’s naming the kind of culture he believes in. A rally, a teacher dance, a basketball game… these aren’t distractions from learning. In his eyes, they are learning. They build connection. They model joy. They show students what it looks like when adults invest in community.
🪴 He models what it means to participate fully.
By dancing, laughing, and playing alongside his colleagues and students, Mike positions himself as more than an instructor—he’s a co-creator of the learning environment. His actions send a powerful message: school is not just about content; it’s about contribution, energy, and shared experience.
🧠 He trusts in the value of what school can be.
Mike’s surprise that some people view school as optional is layered with care and conviction. He knows that when people come together with purpose and heart, “cool stuff happens.” That belief in the potential of school—when driven by people who care deeply—is both hopeful and urgent.

💬 Quote to Hold On To
“Man, I was feeling like—people who think that school is not something they should send their kids to… really surprises me.”
This single sentence reflects both heartbreak and hope. It reveals Mike’s deep belief in what school offers—and a quiet challenge to the systems and stories that might suggest otherwise.

🌈 What Might Be Emerging for Mike
A deepened appreciation for school as a space where joy and learning are intertwined


A strengthened sense of community as a vital part of teaching


A growing clarity that learning isn’t just about subjects—it’s about relationships, participation, and purpose



🌀 Invitations for Reflection or Team Dialogue
How do we make joy visible in our school communities—and why does that matter?


What are we modeling for students about belonging, connection, and participation?


How might we tell a more complete story of what learning looks like in our school?', 'idea', '2025-03-28 15:29:39.131636+00', '2025-03-31 19:55:41.444586+00', 1, 1),
	('5b08211f-5c08-422b-a5c2-ea6e8191a1b7', 'ea155ac4-eb3a-4e24-a4be-84f0143f4348', NULL, 'Learning in Motion: Erica & Mike''s Conversation on AI, Body Intelligence, and Trust
In a brief but layered Upduo session, Erica Crane and Mike Mendelson unpack more than just AI tools—they open a window into the textures of human learning, agency, and uncertainty in the face of technological change.
Embodied Learning & Identity
The conversation kicks off with Mike asking, “What are you getting better at?” Erica’s response is disarmingly grounded—not in teaching or tech, but in bike skills. She contrasts her fluidity in water with her still-developing control on a bike, evoking a rich metaphor for embodied learning. This moment becomes a powerful reflection on identity—how we carry confidence from one domain (swimming) and work to transfer it into another (biking), echoing the Principle Educational Frameworks'' idea of positioning learners as capable and building on existing strengths.
"In swimming… I can navigate as if I am a part of water... but when you watch me on a bike, you''re like, oh." – Erica
Play, Practice, and the Long Game of Expertise
Mike brings in a childhood image—“ripping around our little towns on our tiny BMX bikes”—highlighting the joyful, communal side of mastery. It’s a subtle affirmation of the 10,000-hour rule, but with a human twist: it’s not just about time, it’s about connection, challenge, and curiosity. Their banter honors informal, peer-based learning—a core tenet of Sideby’s platform.
Skepticism and Curiosity Toward AI
When Erica turns the conversation to AI’s role in education, Mike is candid: “Nothing. I’m not really using it… AI is a little spooky.” What could have been a shutdown becomes an opening. Erica doesn’t push—she invites: “Do you mind if I ask what spooks you the most?” This move reflects an asset-based stance; she treats resistance as a site for reflection, not deficit.
Mike’s fears echo popular culture ("You’ve seen Terminator, right?") and deeper concerns about human complacency. But he also recognizes the paradox: educators do guide learners—manipulate in the best sense—to help them grow. He wants AI in the hands of teachers, not advertisers. His resistance is not rejection—it’s a call for intentionality.
A Moment of Real-Life AI Ambiguity
Erica shares a strange interaction—an eerie, possibly AI-mediated calendar snafu. She''s fascinated and frustrated. Her trust is shaken, yet her curiosity is piqued. Her voice carries both irritation as a human and excitement as a learner. This duality captures what many educators feel in this moment of AI emergence: awe and caution intertwined.
“I was excited as an AI learner and really annoyed as a human person.” – Erica
She questions authenticity—not as a binary, but as a spectrum. What does it mean to “connect” in a world where voices, identities, and agents may be simulated?
Themes Emerging
Identity-in-Transition: Both educators reveal how they''re actively reshaping who they are—whether on a bike or in tech.


Trust & Authenticity: The AI moment demands new norms for trust. Erica’s story shows how missteps can undermine even promising tools.


Power of Peer Dialogue: This conversation models what the Sideby platform aims for—honest, generative co-thinking that blends personal and professional growth.


Pedagogical Reframing: Mike reframes manipulation as guidance—a provocative, necessary shift for AI-era teaching.', 'idea', '2025-03-26 23:04:38.886853+00', '2025-03-31 20:27:24.530007+00', 2, NULL),
	('c0671052-4258-41d4-9fce-8fdaffea0b31', 'db53cc1a-13b8-41ef-bc7c-401cd5851e20', NULL, 'test idea', 'idea', '2025-04-04 21:42:42.085808+00', '2025-04-04 21:42:42.085808+00', NULL, NULL),
