	('3c3b97c8-ab03-4802-a0fa-9a800567513c', '0da1de28-4910-465c-a5f3-0e6d3f975b5e', NULL, 'Cass Marketos – Who’s Who Entry
Cass Marketos enters the Sideby experience not as a teacher, but as a self-described “lifelong learner” navigating the tools, prompts, and structure with curiosity and humor. In her words, she''s “kind of troubleshooting [her] way through this with Jeff live,” blending real-time exploration with a playful self-awareness.

While she resists formal prompts and declarations, Cass shares a deeply reflective stance on learning:

“The role of the teacher...is more about giving them frameworks for curiosity versus giving them information to memorize.”
“The role of the learner is attention...attention doesn''t mean agreement, it doesn''t mean subservience. It just means listening with an open mind.”

Cass brings a grounded perspective shaped by embodied experience—her love of composting becomes a metaphor for transformation, beauty, and mindful presence:

“Turning a bunch of junk into something vital and full of life. It''s just completely beautiful and I love it.”

Even in a brief reflection, Cass offers rich insights about agency, openness, and the kinds of learning that feel human, alive, and deeply felt.', 'idea', '2025-04-18 00:06:59.772414+00', '2025-04-18 00:06:59.772414+00', NULL, NULL),
	('5f0490b7-5726-4d9e-aefe-8424540c071a', 'd8e9db8f-f716-4f11-9fb9-c204d951fd7d', NULL, '🧠 Differentiation as Care: Katie’s ELL Story
For Katie Stahl, stepping into her new role teaching a sheltered U.S. History class for English Language Learners wasn’t just another course prep—it was a bold experiment. “This is the first year having that. So… that is totally an experiment,” she shared, her voice balancing realism and genuine excitement. “And I love it. It’s great.”

But what made it possible—what kept it from tipping into overwhelm—was her strategic use of AI. With students ranging from WIDA level 1.5 (just a few months in the U.S., with minimal English) to level 4.5 (more fluent, but still acquiring academic English), the range of language proficiency in her room was vast.

“I have to provide different levels of content for them because of that, and AI has made that more possible,” she explained.

Her approach? She takes the standard U.S. History curriculum, and through AI tools, reduces it first to a 6th grade reading level, and then again to a 2nd or 3rd grade level—not to simplify the ideas, but to make sure every student has a meaningful on-ramp into the content.

“Otherwise, this class would be so much harder,” she says plainly—not as a complaint, but as a truth many teachers quietly carry.

What Katie is doing here isn’t just translation. It’s translation of access, translation of dignity. She’s refusing to default to a lowest-common-denominator model. Instead, she’s crafting multiple doorways into the same learning goals, scaffolding rigor with precision and empathy.

And it’s not theoretical—it’s working.

“AI has made teaching this class possible.”

There’s agency in that statement. Not just “made it easier,” or “saved time,” but made it possible. Katie is using AI not as a gimmick, but as an equity engine, giving each learner the chance to engage with real content at the level that matches their readiness. She’s the architect of the structure; AI is her scaffold.', 'idea', '2025-03-26 21:21:11.667682+00', '2025-03-27 12:43:20.143542+00', 3, 3),
	('b147a190-64b3-44f4-a437-bec6d3616d74', 'db53cc1a-13b8-41ef-bc7c-401cd5851e20', NULL, 'Jeff Crawford: A Facilitator of Growth, a Champion of Learner Agency
In a field that often prizes certainty, Jeff Crawford embraces the beautiful uncertainty of learning. "I think of myself as one who enjoys getting people to think and talk about things," Jeff shares. It''s not just talk—it''s meaningful discourse, the kind that moves people, deepens understanding, and grows community. For Jeff, education is a living, breathing act of co-creation. His journey offers a model of what it looks like when an educator keeps learning right alongside others.

Once a classroom teacher and now a learning experience facilitator working with adults, Jeff has carried one core belief with him: every learner is capable. "The role of the teacher," he explains, "is to create a safe place where learners feel they are positioned as capable and that they have identity and agency that’s valuable"​.

What makes Jeff’s story compelling isn’t just what he teaches—it’s how he shows up in the work. In conversations, he is reflective, curious, and grounded in humility. "I’m still on my quest to facilitate meaningful discourse," he admits. That honest striving reveals the heart of a growth-oriented educator. He’s not looking for quick fixes; he’s looking to build spaces where learners lead, connect, and thrive.

One of Jeff’s standout moments came as he described his shift from student-facing to adult-facing learning. The transition required him to reimagine how to cultivate dialogue and trust in online, asynchronous formats. "How do I facilitate meaningful discourse… with adults all over the place?" he wondered aloud. That inquiry became a driver for innovation. Rather than center himself as the expert, Jeff focused on scaffolding others to shine: “How do I get out of the way and just give them the tools to do the things they need to grow?”​

His facilitative stance, his neuroscience curiosity, and his belief in the collective wisdom of educators inform everything he does—including his work on tech tools that make educator reflection more visible and accessible. He talks about automating transcripts, pulling insights, and returning “artifacts” back to educators not as data points, but as stories. “Instead of you trying to put all the stuff together, we want to do it for you—so you can focus on the conversation,” he tells a colleague. In that one sentence, Jeff offers a glimpse of his values: elevate others, simplify the burden, amplify the joy​.

Jeff Crawford is not just learning how to lead with technology—he’s learning how to humanize it. His story invites all of us to consider: What if we measured our growth not by what we say, but by what we make possible for others?', 'idea', '2025-04-04 21:47:58.62817+00', '2025-04-04 21:47:58.62817+00', NULL, NULL),
