	('72a95130-40f5-408f-9883-be0993923c9c', '4e8fa647-0aad-4962-a879-826b7edc6dd4', NULL, 'Jeff Crawford: Cultivating Learning Through Connection, Curiosity, and Courage
When Jeff Crawford introduces himself, he doesn’t begin with titles. He starts with what drives him: a deep love for facilitating learning. A self-described “budding neuroscientist,” Jeff approaches education with reverence for the brain’s complexity and a commitment to nurturing growth at every stage of life. But it’s not just content he’s after—it’s connection, identity, and transformation.

Across the sessions, Jeff emerges as an educator who is both philosophically grounded and relentlessly practical. His core stance is clear: “Each learner is capable of figuring things out on their own. The role of the teacher is to create a safe place… where they are positioned as capable, and where they have an identity and agency that’s valuable.”​

He speaks often of facilitating meaningful discourse, reflecting on how his journey has shifted from classroom-based work with students to virtual and hybrid spaces with adult learners. Even after years of experience, Jeff shares openly about the challenges and learning curves: “I was getting better at [facilitating] in the classroom and I was stoked about that. But… how do you do that with adults? How do you use these routines to engage learners?”​

Technology is not just a tool in Jeff’s practice—it’s a co-thinker. Whether exploring AI platforms like Lovable, Notebook LM, or Perplexity, or helping educators integrate scheduling tools and transcription apps, he stays on the edge of innovation with humility and a spirit of experimentation. He models growth mindset vividly, even joking: “I’m like level 2 out of 4. Not this noob, but I played around…”​

But what truly sets Jeff apart is his heart for community and storytelling. He dreams of helping educators offload cognitive burdens by turning transcripts into rich portfolios, believing that tools should let people “enjoy the conversation” and that “out of it will come… your cool podcast or whatever down the road.”​

There’s also a vulnerable honesty to his reflection: how he’s unlearning rigid beliefs from his upbringing, or how his art—like his doodles and woodworking—helps him make sense of the world and stay well. His imagined AI superpower? A tool that monitors his well-being like a “kindergarten mom” would, reminding him to walk, reflect, and recharge​.

In every conversation, Jeff models what it looks like to learn out loud—to be both guide and learner, innovator and listener, builder and believer. His presence reminds us that education at its best is deeply human, beautifully messy, and endlessly full of possibility.', 'idea', '2025-04-07 21:56:02.807452+00', '2025-04-07 21:56:02.807452+00', NULL, NULL),
	('a2f3b93b-2d83-4fac-830b-58265831711d', 'd3be9b68-297b-416b-828a-eabddea74cb7', NULL, 'this is a new idea at 9:27am', 'idea', '2025-04-15 13:28:03.568184+00', '2025-04-15 13:28:03.568184+00', NULL, NULL),
	('7f780f1e-9727-466b-9abe-e8c2bed78420', '43812edc-e7fd-4226-bb9c-cd9bd174e0b0', NULL, 'Mike Peck’s Journey
Mike Peck brings to the table a sharp blend of technological fluency, systems-level thinking, and a curiosity-driven approach to learning. He doesn’t just use AI—he wields it as an extension of his workflow, using tools like ChatGPT to synthesize data, shape strategy, and move from insight to action faster and more thoughtfully. His approach reflects both a personal fascination with the future of knowledge work and a broader ambition to support education systems in evolving toward more meaningful and personalized experiences.
What sets Mike apart is his comfort with ambiguity paired with a bias toward experimentation. He operates with the mindset that “AI won’t replace us, but people who know how to use it well will outpace those who don’t.” His references to Zero to One and Abundance reflect a futurist optimism, yet he’s also grounded—keenly aware of the need to build tools and structures that serve real people, not just shiny prototypes.
In conversation, Mike consistently:
Positions learners as capable by asking how technology can amplify rather than replace human insight.


Enhances identity and agency by modeling transparency in his own process—openly sharing not just polished results but how he iterates with AI and refines his thinking.


Values multiple contributions by seeking out cross-disciplinary perspectives, especially from those outside the tech field, to test his ideas and build better mental models.


Mike is early in his journey of translating this tech-forward stance into frameworks that are legible and useful for educators at all levels of AI readiness. He recognizes the risk of “solutionism” and is actively working to build bridges—crafting resources and reflective tools that help others make sense of AI on their own terms, not just his.

Three Pathway Challenges for Mike
Design for Diverse Entry Points: How might Mike co-create tools that serve both power users like himself and educators who are just beginning their AI journey?


Slow Down to Go Far: How might he intentionally pause to document and model his process in ways that make it visible, learnable, and transferable?


Build Relational Infrastructure: How might Mike shift from just sharing outputs to designing collaborative routines that invite others into the “how” behind his practice?', 'idea', '2025-04-15 21:35:24.206625+00', '2025-04-15 21:35:24.206625+00', NULL, NULL),
