	('c283dc1c-fbd0-4135-b969-e5daae5a73f4', '43812edc-e7fd-4226-bb9c-cd9bd174e0b0', NULL, 'Beyond the Slide Deck: Mike and Jeff on AI, Learning, and the Urgency of Imagination
Date: April 15, 2025
Duration: 33 minutes
Participants: Mike Peck & Jeff (Masterwoot)
Topic Threads: #ai_learning_tools #project_management #learner_agency #dreamcamps

The Moment of Shift
For both Mike and Jeff, the road to redefining teaching started when the tools of the trade outgrew the traditional script.

Mike, a Pennsylvania-based technology leader and educator, traces his transformation to a pivotal moment in 2010 when he was asked to lead his district’s online learning efforts. What followed was a shift away from “slide decks and worksheets” and toward gamification, experimentation, and the builder’s mindset. “Learning through play and exploration was more powerful than anything else I had done,” he reflected.

Jeff’s inflection point came earlier, in 2005, while designing an online Algebra course for Spokane Virtual Learning. It wasn’t just the shift to digital—it was the existential question it surfaced: “How do you facilitate learning when students have Google, Mathematica, and emulators at home?” That tension drove him out of his “classic high school math teacher” identity and into a space where facilitation, curiosity, and design thinking became central.

AI as Assistant, Archive, and Amplifier
Both educators now treat AI like an essential collaborator.

“When I’m driving, I talk to ChatGPT to reflect on the day or dump ideas. It’s like having a cognitive amplifier in my pocket.” – Mike

Jeff agreed, describing his evolving use of assistants for capturing reflections, ideating, and synthesizing big thoughts. “I used to wish I could hire a grad student to ride along with me, take notes, and think with me. Now I just use my phone,” he said.

Mike shared how he’s organizing his AI reflections into project folders and is even designing a bespoke Pomodoro-based task management tool tailored to his own workflow. His vision? An AI-integrated life planner that can track ideas, generate insights, and keep pace with his rapid cycles of thinking.

The Future Role of Learners: Dreamers and Project Managers
The conversation’s heart centered on a redefinition of learning roles. As AI rapidly commoditizes knowledge and even aspects of intelligence, both agreed that “learners must become dreamers and managers of their own learning.”

“We do a terrible job of cultivating creativity and dreaming in schools,” Mike said. “We need to teach learners to be problem seekers. To dream things into existence.”

Jeff echoed the call with stories from his own “dream camp” and emphasized the increasing importance of executive functioning in a world where information—and even ideas—are abundant. “There’s too much now, even from our AI assistants. Managing your own dreams is the next skill. What’s worth your time? What’s real?”

Key Insights from the Session
AI Changes the Nature of Reflection: Voice-first journaling, asynchronous processing, and intelligent summarization expand the cognitive space for educators and learners alike.

Agency is the New Literacy: The ability to navigate, prioritize, and act amidst abundance is replacing the ability to retain or regurgitate.

Designing Tools vs. Consuming Them: Mike’s drive to build custom AI tools for tech leaders mirrors the broader need for educators to become not just users but shapers of AI.

Transparency Builds Belonging: When educators share their learning process, struggles, and “wobbly moments,” it invites learners into the messy, beautiful act of learning itself.

Potential Action Steps
Dream Camps for Educators and Students
Co-design immersive sessions that prioritize creative play, AI-enabled ideation, and speculative problem-solving.

AI Journaling as a Practice
Support educators in using voice-to-text and AI assistants to capture real-time reflections and transform them into structured insights.

Build Your Own Tool Challenges
Encourage educators to identify real problems in their day-to-day and use platforms like Lovable to prototype AI-supported solutions.

Peer Learning Pods
Facilitate 20-minute focused co-working sessions where educators reflect, build, or iterate together—much like Jeff’s offer to “just co-think with you and ask questions.”

“We’re no longer the smartest species on the planet. Intelligence is abundant. What do we do with that?” – Mike Peck

“We’re human. We’re learners. That’s the constant we can keep building from.” – Jeff', 'idea', '2025-04-15 21:49:40.616135+00', '2025-04-15 22:03:01.607534+00', 1, NULL),
