	('e0d2ef0c-5a47-4a15-ac43-4aeda33787ef', '0da1de28-4910-465c-a5f3-0e6d3f975b5e', NULL, 'Cass Marketos – Quarter-Page Learner Insight
“I just have such raw dialogue brain.”

Cass’s first peer session with Jeff offers a compelling window into her learning style and evolving stance on technology, teaching, and educational values. With disarming honesty, she shares how generative AI has shifted a long-standing barrier:

“A huge part of my job is creating structured content plans… and my brain has always struggled to organize information. I just want to talk to people… but now I can do a raw thought dump and ask AI to put it into a structured format for me.”​upduo-session-138134-20…

This shift reflects more than efficiency—it’s a reorientation of agency. For Cass, AI becomes not just a tool but a collaborative partner, enabling her to express complex ideas with clarity, especially when her thinking is nonlinear or emotionally embedded.

She’s deeply attuned to how learning environments feel—especially the tone and motive behind how information is shared. She is wary of persuasion and values intellectual humility:

“If I feel manipulated by someone, I immediately just don’t believe anything they say… but if someone presents something with humility—‘this is what I think, and here’s why’—I’m very receptive.”​upduo-session-138134-20…

Cass also surfaces critical values around surveillance, agency, and student motivation. When presented with a scenario involving an AI engagement-monitoring tool in classrooms, she reacts strongly:

“That’s such a terrible value system… I want students to pay attention because learning is joyful and they see the benefits in their lives—not because they’re being watched.”​

She shows openness to rethinking her assumptions when Jeff models a reflective, questioning approach. That shift becomes a moment of learning she names explicitly:

“That was a powerful learning moment for me… always ask questions and always think about the whole thing.”​

🛤️ Three Pathway Challenges Cass Might Explore
Design for Dialogue: How might Cass co-design learning or collaboration spaces that embrace raw thought, emotional honesty, and voice-first reflection—especially for others who share her nonlinear learning mode?

Rhetoric & Reception: How might Cass experiment with presenting ideas in ways that balance authenticity with care for how others receive and interpret them—especially across lines of difference?

Tech for Agency: How might Cass help shape ethical, empowering uses of AI that preserve learner autonomy while expanding access and capacity?', 'idea', '2025-04-18 00:09:38.441763+00', '2025-04-18 00:09:38.441763+00', NULL, NULL),
	('5c6fa85f-d6bc-4f5e-ad50-5b478db203cb', '4e8fa647-0aad-4962-a879-826b7edc6dd4', NULL, '“Wake Up, Folks”: Rethinking Teaching and Bias in the Age of AI
April 17, 2025
 Co-authored by Jeff Crawford and Insight Fly 2025, an AI-powered assistant.
 Grounded in the conversation of Tom Thorpe and Jeff Crawford
In a 30-minute Sideby session that wove together storytelling, challenge, laughter, and serious reflection, two educators—Tom Thorpe and Jeff Crawford—stepped into the heart of a conversation many are just beginning to navigate: What does AI mean for the identity, agency, and craft of teaching?
From the very beginning, their stories signaled this wasn’t going to be a typical tech talk. This was a co-thinking space, where AI was examined not just for what it can do, but for what it reveals about what educators value.

Enhancing Identity and Agency: A Different Kind of AI Story
One of the most compelling moments in the conversation came when Tom recounted helping a student—a senior with a complex home life and a strong internal drive—use ChatGPT to write a business plan. This wasn’t for a grade or assignment. It was deeply personal. Her goal? To impress her dad.
Rather than approach the situation with suspicion or concern about “cheating,” Tom recognized what was really at stake: the student’s identity, her longing to be seen, and her right to shape how she showed up in that relationship. He reframed the task, internally thinking: “Let’s ChatGPT the hell out of this so you can impress your dad.”
What followed wasn’t just a polished plan—it was a catalyst for conversation between the student and her father, including discussions about college. It became a moment of visibility, confidence, and empowerment.
Tom’s approach underscores a critical insight: when educators prioritize connection, context, and care, AI becomes a tool for deepening student identity—not diminishing it. He wasn’t outsourcing the learning. He was leveraging the tool to support a bigger human need: to feel seen, taken seriously, and full of potential.
As Tom recalled, “It helped her have a great conversation with her dad... and he was like, oh, you''re for real, like... you could actually go and do stuff.” The technology wasn''t the point. The transformation was.

AI Won’t Replace Us—Unless We Do the Real Work
As the session unfolded, both educators confronted a familiar tension: fear that AI will replace teachers. But instead of brushing it off or rushing to reassure, they examined the roots of that fear.
Tom put it bluntly: “If you think you''re going to get replaced by ChatGPT... if that''s the only thing that we do as teachers... then yes, that probably would be replaced.”
He wasn’t referring to the heart of teaching—building relationships, sparking thinking, cultivating curiosity—but to the kinds of tasks that are mechanical, surface-level, and easily scripted. If our definition of teaching is reduced to those routines, replacement becomes not just possible, but logical.
The fear, in his view, wasn’t really about AI—it was about undervaluing the real complexity and humanity of the profession.
Jeff added nuance, noting how even teachers themselves can inadvertently position their roles around low-level, repetitive tasks: “If our jobs are allocated and our roles and the things that we do are menial, repetitive tasks... then we''re not being creative or we''re not being human.”
Both agreed: if what educators do can be fully programmed, then it probably should be. But that''s not what powerful teaching looks like.
“Wake up, folks.” Tom said. “That is not your job, and it''s never been our job.”

Seeing Bias: In AI, In Ourselves, In Our Systems
The conversation took a reflective turn as they examined bias—not just in AI tools, but in their own classrooms. Some biases are overt, but others are hidden in plain sight.
Jeff shared a story about asking a GPT model to adapt a thoughtfully designed math lesson. Despite the lesson’s strong foundations, the AI defaulted to beginning with summary and pre-teaching—choices that undermined learner agency. “The GPT said pre-teach and preload, which totally took the position of the learners... and made them passive,” he recalled.
This wasn’t AI hallucination—it was bias embedded in training data. Common practice had been mistaken for best practice.
“Bias isn''t just about race or gender,” Jeff noted. “It''s about bad pedagogy becoming authority because it''s common.”
Tom picked up the thread, imagining AI not as a generator but as a kind of analytic mirror. He envisioned a classroom discussion going sideways, and an AI tool helping to visually trace the thread—highlighting where the conversation drifted, which metaphors dominated, or why certain students disengaged.
They didn’t frame AI as a content engine, but as a reflective co-facilitator—capable of helping educators notice the deeper structures of discourse: Who spoke? Who was left out? What language signaled inclusion—or exclusion?
As Tom reflected, “Why might Jeff not be talking? Well, because we were like only giving sports, like basketball metaphors. Jeff doesn''t know shit about basketball... So let’s go back, let’s pull out a different metaphor.”
This was a vision of AI in service of awareness, not automation.

Teaching Isn’t Simple—And That’s the Point
As their conversation deepened, Tom and Jeff returned to a fundamental truth: teaching is not simple work—and it never has been.
What makes the profession irreplaceable is precisely its complexity. It’s relational, contextual, and deeply human. Yet too often, systems and narratives flatten it into something mechanical. When educators begin to internalize the belief that their value lies in delivering steps, checking boxes, or managing compliance, the fear of being replaced by AI feels not only possible—but inevitable.
Tom captured the heart of that concern: “If we want kids to be simple and we want teachers to be simple, then we’re just perpetuating that fear and locking it in.” Jeff echoed the risk of dehumanization: “Then we''re not being creative or we''re not being human.”
Both challenged the idea that mastery means removing struggle or complexity. In fact, they argued the opposite: good teaching invites complexity, honors student agency, and builds the conditions for students to wrestle, wonder, and grow.
Tom called it out when Jeff described the moment when his GPT assistant, trained on equity-centered frameworks, still reverted to a common instructional myth—pre-teaching and preloading content. Tom pointed out how this reflects how AI often mirrors existing practice, whether it’s good or not. “You''re doing that thing,” Tom said, referencing the impulse to over-script learning. The danger wasn’t just that the tool was wrong—but that it mimicked what’s already too common.
Ultimately, both educators emphasized that the real threat isn’t AI—it’s the temptation to reduce teaching to something predictable. As Tom put it: “The fear is that teachers don''t value what they do.”

✏️ Three Practical Actions Tom or Jeff Might Take Using AI Tools
1. Craft a Reflective LinkedIn Post from This Conversation
Use this transcript with an AI assistant to write a short post sharing your insights on AI, bias, and the irreplaceable value of teaching. Title prompt: “What AI Can’t Replace” or “The Fear Behind the Fear.”
2. Build a Bias Mapping Protocol
Use an AI tool to help develop a protocol that maps classroom dialogue: Who speaks? What metaphors are used? What biases might be surfacing? Pair it with real-time classroom transcripts or recordings to build teacher and student reflection.
3. Co-create a GPT With Students
Instead of using a generic AI model, use an assistant to co-build a custom GPT with your students—trained on your class values, equity commitments, and preferred teaching practices. Call it Bobby, as Jeff joked, and let it reflect your learning community.

If this article sparked ideas or reflections of your own, we invite you to carry the conversation forward—whether through your next AI interaction, classroom dialogue, or faculty meeting. Because, as Tom and Jeff reminded us, our work is not simple, and that’s exactly why it matters.


"The answer is clear—and complicated. What we''ve been forgetting to include in our designs is our own weaknesses—not to exploit them (which many have done) but to respond to them (which most have not)."
 —Scott Doorley and Carissa Carter, Assembling Tomorrow', 'idea', '2025-04-18 00:10:05.972559+00', '2025-04-18 00:10:05.972559+00', NULL, NULL),
	('ab778825-21e1-4adc-a1aa-eb768e075f89', '78789bc7-3f6a-484d-96de-fe42c3c9c48e', NULL, 'Vanessa Edwards
Career Connector | Systems Shifter | Dream Implementer

Vanessa brings her whole self to her work—an energetic, systems-savvy, and equity-driven leader who thrives at the intersection of possibility and pragmatism. As Edmonds School District’s Career Connected Learning Coordinator, she builds real-world bridges for middle and high school students through internships, career tours, job shadows, and project-based learning. Her commitment to student-centered pathways and community outcomes is evident in every role she takes on.

Three ideas to think with:

“Connections that create a positive outcome in communities and systems” – Vanessa isn’t just designing opportunities for students, she’s reshaping the systems they move through.

“I am a jack of all trades and love to implement and make dreams come to life” – Her gift isn’t only visioning, it’s doing. Vanessa thrives in the complexity of cross-sector work and is energized by seeing ideas come to fruition.

“Launching the EDit implementation team to support school districts in improving outcomes and systems” – She’s actively scaling her leadership, moving beyond local coordination to a broader statewide role influencing educational transformation.

This is a leader who believes in equity, operates from a deep sense of purpose, and brings both strategic insight and roll-up-your-sleeves action to the work. Expect her to ask bold questions—and to back it up with practical tools for getting things done.', 'idea', '2025-04-19 20:26:06.035441+00', '2025-04-19 20:26:06.035441+00', NULL, NULL),
	('74dc8f70-dcbe-46d7-a3f5-6999f039acee', '4bb175fe-ebd9-4641-9edc-593037b6152d', NULL, 'Michelle O’Shea
Science Teacher, Instructional Coach, Crew Advisor — California

“The job of a teacher is to ask great questions… and to model curiosity for students.”

Michelle enters this learning experience as a seasoned educator with over 20 years of classroom and leadership experience. Deeply rooted in expeditionary learning, she now finds her greatest professional joy in coaching fellow teachers, serving as an instructional guide to improve practice across her school. Her time is split between science instruction, crew advising, and leading adult learning—with a clear passion for lifting others up.

Michelle’s north star in this process is learning how to better leverage AI to support both her own practice and, more importantly, the teachers she mentors. She hopes AI can become a tool to free up cognitive and emotional space for teachers, helping them direct more energy toward student-centered work. She’s curious about what’s possible, but grounded in the real challenges educators face—especially around time and burnout.

Three quotes to hold onto:

“I want to understand the capabilities of [AI] in order to free up some capacity for teachers.”

“Students should be the ones working hard. They should be the ones talking—especially to each other.”

“When I’m in the kitchen, cooking with a show on and space to myself—that’s my flow.”', 'idea', '2025-04-19 20:28:58.320697+00', '2025-04-19 20:28:58.320697+00', NULL, NULL),
	('88dd2895-803b-4f85-861b-7ac89ecdfa65', '4bb175fe-ebd9-4641-9edc-593037b6152d', NULL, 'Extended Learner Story: Michelle O’Shea
From Craft to Clarity: Reclaiming Time, Voice, and Collective Purpose

Michelle O’Shea brings the precision of a curriculum designer and the reflective mindset of a learner to her current role—and this conversation with Jeff revealed just how powerfully those elements are intersecting. As she navigates a year abroad in Spain, remote coaching responsibilities, and a major curriculum development lift, Michelle is recalibrating what “good work” means—and how much of herself she can sustainably give.

Her recent efforts writing yearlong science courses for an online school have revealed the potential of AI tools not just to save time, but to amplify clarity and unlock deeper learning. “I don’t have to get so hung up on every single word,” she reflected. “I can give myself permission to get my ideas out and then use the tool that’s available—‘Hey, polish this for me.’” By loosening her grip on perfection, Michelle is not settling for less; she’s making room for more—more creativity, more human connection, more balance.

But this growth isn’t without tension. A self-described “public learner with reservations,” Michelle is weighing whether to return to her long-time school or take a new role as a founding teacher at the online school she’s helping launch. “Do I want to sign up to be bad at something for a while?” she asked. “I like to be a learner—but I don’t like to be such a public learner.” Her honesty cuts through the noise of “growth mindset” clichés—revealing the very real vulnerability that educators carry when they shift roles or take on new challenges in public view.

And yet, she also knows the stakes are bigger than her own comfort. The possibility of creating high-quality, authentic curriculum for more learners—especially in under-resourced or nontraditional settings—is part of what draws her forward. “Anything we can do to make this work a little easier for teachers… is really important,” she shared. “It’s going to keep people in the profession longer.”

What emerged in this conversation wasn’t just a reflection on tools or transitions—it was a portrait of Michelle as a bridge-builder: someone who can hold the tension between fidelity and flexibility, who thinks deeply about how to support both learners and colleagues, and who is wrestling—beautifully—with the next version of her educator identity.

Three Possible Action Pathways
🔹 1. Promote Herself: Michelle could begin more openly naming and sharing the strategic value she brings—as a curriculum developer, systems thinker, and mentor who bridges content rigor with relational depth. Whether through LinkedIn, sideby, or future collaborations, she might claim her own “thought leader” space, not as self-promotion, but as honest testimony to a powerful and sustainable model of educator leadership.

🔹 2. Lift the Identity and Voice of Others: Michelle’s keen eye for coaching and curriculum gives her a platform to help others—especially overwhelmed or under-mentored teachers—see their own potential more clearly. She might lead with questions like: What’s your “good enough” this week? or How might AI free up time for deeper connection with your students? Her perspective reframes efficiency as a form of care.

🔹 3. Advance the Community Good: With her background in founding a school and mentoring colleagues, Michelle is poised to shape the systems that sustain educator well-being and student learning. Whether through building advisory structures, co-authoring shared resources, or influencing school design, her next step could focus on how to build communities of practice where educator agency, curiosity, and joy are not just encouraged—but expected.', 'idea', '2025-04-21 17:49:02.347564+00', '2025-04-21 17:49:02.347564+00', NULL, NULL),
	('16f5dd93-fd8f-4589-ac08-6e879cf64afb', '4bb175fe-ebd9-4641-9edc-593037b6152d', NULL, 'Good Enough, Together: A Conversation Between Michelle O’Shea & Jeff Crawford
“We want to continuously improve… but at some point, you’ve got to find your good enough point and give yourself permission to move on.” —Michelle O’Shea

In a time when education is awash in innovation but short on stillness, Michelle O’Shea and Jeff Crawford’s conversation offered something rare: grounded wisdom and human connection.

Their 34-minute exchange wasn’t just about AI, curriculum, or coaching. It was about what it means to do meaningful work in this moment—and how the right conversation, at the right time, can shift the way we see ourselves and what’s possible.

From Overwhelm to Permission
Michelle, joining from the southern coast of Spain where she’s spending a second year abroad, brought insight from a dual role: curriculum designer for a new online school and longtime instructional coach for a California charter. Her candor set the tone early. “I can get so bogged down in the details,” she admitted, reflecting on years of holding herself to unnecessarily high standards. But the shift is happening. “You’ve got to find your ‘good enough’ point,” she said. “And give yourself permission to move on.”

Jeff—an experienced math educator, facilitator, and curriculum strategist—picked up that thread and carried it back later with humility and humor. “You don’t have to explore all the ends of the universe, Jeff,” he laughed. “Just pick a path and go play.” Together, they named a familiar tension in the profession: the desire to do it all, and the real cost of trying.

AI as an Amplifier, Not a Shortcut
Both educators agreed that AI isn’t a shortcut or a threat—it’s a catalyst. A new kind of assistant that can save time, refine thinking, and enhance quality when used with discernment.

Michelle shared how she’s using tools like ChatGPT to source relevant anchor texts and research articles for her curriculum work. “It’s changing my work in both efficiency and probably quality as well,” she explained. “I’m putting in the same effort—but getting more out of it.”

Jeff likened the impact to finding a new gear on a bike: “We have turbo boosters on our work, or like a new gear for pedaling—it’s the same effort, but you go farther.”

Michelle extended the metaphor, thinking not just of teachers, but of students too:

“I love that—turbo boosters or easier gear to pedal in—because this is all about what kids are getting out of this too. If you’re going to create really dynamic, meaningful, authentic curriculum… anything we can do to make that a little easier is really important.”

The gear shift isn’t about taking the easy route. It’s about making the uphill ride possible—for teachers and learners alike.

Beyond Tools: The Value of Peer Conversation
Michelle and Jeff didn’t just exchange reflections—they sharpened one another’s thinking, step by step, in a way that only authentic dialogue can. This was learning-in-the-moment: not scripted, not performative, but real.

Michelle named a core struggle—balancing the pressure to produce high-quality work with a desire to be more present, more human. Jeff reflected that back and offered language that helped her frame it more generously. Michelle then shared how AI tools were helping her loosen her grip on perfection while improving her craft. Jeff brought metaphor, she brought context. He asked questions, she processed out loud. Together, they built meaning.

Even their exploration of AI’s impact on students was collaborative. Michelle acknowledged she was still thinking it through. Jeff didn’t jump to conclusions—he held space, listened, and added a lens from math practice standards: “Use appropriate tools strategically.” Michelle connected instantly, realizing how relevant that thinking was for her science curriculum and learners.

Both educators were learners in this moment—not performing expertise, but co-constructing it.

This kind of exchange—where two people think better and do better because of each other—is rare. And it models exactly the kind of conditions we hope to create in schools: spaces where vulnerability and agency coexist, and where reflection isn’t a solo act, but a shared endeavor.

In a system that often isolates educators or expects them to show up as fully formed experts, this kind of peer dialogue is a quiet revolution. It doesn’t just affirm where we are—it nudges us forward, together.

Why This Matters
As sideby.ai continues to connect educators across roles, geographies, and levels of AI fluency, this conversation is a glimpse into what’s possible: thoughtful reflection, practical insight, and authentic growth—all in under 40 minutes, from opposite sides of the world.

Michelle and Jeff didn’t just talk about teaching. They practiced learning—with each other. And in doing so, they reminded us that the future of educator growth isn’t just in the tools we use. It’s in the conversations we choose to have—and the people we choose to become through them.

Suggested tags: #EducatorVoices #AIinEducation #PeerLearning #HumanCenteredAI #CurriculumDesign #InstructionalCoaching', 'idea', '2025-04-21 17:59:02.53135+00', '2025-04-21 17:59:02.53135+00', NULL, NULL),
